1
00:00:00,294 --> 00:00:05,794
This is QVault: a quantum-safe vault for SOL and SPL tokens on Solana.

2
00:00:06,421 --> 00:00:17,821
On Solana, your address is your Ed25519 public key. It is visible to everyone, and a large enough quantum computer could compute the private key behind it.

3
00:00:18,444 --> 00:00:32,669
QVault keeps funds in a program account with no private key on chain. It opens only with a Winternitz one-time signature. That is hash-based, built on SHA-256, and verified by our program.

4
00:00:33,334 --> 00:00:38,884
Let's try it live on Solana devnet, with the demo wallet that runs in the browser.

5
00:00:40,762 --> 00:00:47,387
First, the browser creates a vault from a random recovery code. That code never leaves this device.

6
00:00:47,739 --> 00:00:51,139
We deposit some devnet SOL from the normal wallet.

7
00:00:54,377 --> 00:00:58,902
And an SPL test token. SOL and tokens sit in the same vault.

8
00:01:04,548 --> 00:01:22,648
Now a withdrawal. The browser computes an 840-byte Winternitz signature. It fixes the recipient, the amount, the token and the next vault. The program re-hashes it on chain. Then permissionless steps pay out, and everything else rotates to a fresh vault under a new key.

9
00:01:24,607 --> 00:01:28,732
The token works the same way, signed with the next one-time key.

10
00:01:31,014 --> 00:01:36,414
Every step is a real devnet transaction, with an explorer link to verify it.

11
00:01:37,038 --> 00:01:47,363
Winternitz vaults already exist on Solana, but they hold SOL only. QVault protects SOL, SPL tokens and Token-2022 in one vault.

12
00:01:47,978 --> 00:02:02,203
The business model is simple: a zero point one percent fee, only on withdrawals to third parties. Moving funds into your own next vault is free. We start with protocol and DAO treasuries, then wallet integrations.

13
00:02:02,820 --> 00:02:17,445
QVault is open source, with attack tests and a devnet pipeline that runs this exact flow in a real browser. It is not audited yet, so it runs on devnet only. An external audit comes before any mainnet launch.

